WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.680
Early on
in your rap career,

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.760
you write about facing
constant criticism.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:04.880
I love this quote.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.800
You say,
"At Catholic school,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.640
no matter how well-spoken
or intelligent,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.560
I was still the Black kid.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.520
In Winnefeld,
no matter how up I was

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.000
on the latest music or fashion,
I was never quite...

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.440
"Black enough."

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.200
I became one
of the first hip-hop artists

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.600
who was considered "safe" enough
for white audiences.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.480
But with Black audiences,
I was labeled "soft"

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.400
because
I wasn't rapping about

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.400
hard-core, gangster stuff.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.000
This racial dynamic is something
that has plagued me

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.040
in various forms
throughout my entire life."

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.000
And I wanted to ask you
how have you navigated

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.920
what you call
this racial dynamic?

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:40.800
So...

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.680
the elixir for me

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.360
has been comedy.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.760
Comedy crosses all divides.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.560
Mm-hmm.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.640
If you learn how to
make people

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.840
laugh, it's like...

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.920
it's the sugar for anything
else that you wanna bring...

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.240
-Mm-hmm.
-to that person.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.000
People are wide open...

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.000
-Open.
-when they're laughing.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:11.600
Right?

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.160
So, for me,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.120
throughout my career,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.440
the...the bigger the laugh,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.160
I realized

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.720
the less I was
the "Black" dude.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:31.680
Mmm...

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.440
Right? The...

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.320
The bigger the laugh,
the less I was the soft dude.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.720
Right?
Laughter trumps everything.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.560
Mmm.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.120
Right?
And it's--it's a magical--

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.440
it's a magical energy
that connects people

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.120
and that it,
for a brief moment, at least,

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.360
-purifies the poisons.
-Yes.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.440
And you're not in any way

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.080
marginalizing or minimizing
overt racism

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.120
because you say,

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.800
"Every encounter I ever had
with overt racism

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.640
were with people I estimated
to be weak enemies at best,

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.360
so overt racism although dangerous
and ever present

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.280
never made me feel inferior."

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.640
I thought that was
so interesting.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.440
-I felt the same way.
-Same way.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.560
So "I was raised to believe

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.920
that you can encounter
any situation racist or otherwise.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.400
Hard work, education and God
will topple

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.080
any and all obstacles
and enemies."

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.920
And I wonder,
how did that affect you

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.360
moving through the world
of power as a Black man?

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:38.160
So...

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.720
It's funny--
Tyrese called me one time.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:45.840
He was goin' through
something.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.880
And he was mad,
he had just left a meeting

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:51.840
or something like that.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.000
And he's like,
"Man, what I gotta do

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.840
"to walk into these meetings

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.080
"and have them not look at me

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.200
as just a *****
walkin' in the office?"

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.320
And I said, "Well, you gotta
stop thinking of yourself

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.680
as a ***** walkin'
in the office."

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:14.640
Right? So...

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.360
I don't--I don't think
of myself like that.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.160
Right? So because I don't
think of myself like that,

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.480
I'm not feeding that in
the person that I encounter...

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:31.840
right?

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.840
But I just think--
I think it's-it's...

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.520
it's really important--
and I was blessed,

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.280
as I talk about it
in the book--

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.440
my encounters with overt racism

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.600
were never with people
I thought were smart or strong.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.840
-Had any power.
-They didn't have any power.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.120
Even-even--there were some
like with police interactions.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.560
One-a the times I got called
***** was from a cop.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.800
-Mm-hmm.
-Right?

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.880
But J.L., beside me,
my-my manager...

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:02.880
had a law degree.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.280
So he said,
"Thank you for that.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.280
We're gonna head
right to Internal Affairs."

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.320
Right? So it's like...

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.760
the concept of intelligence,

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.440
hard work, and God

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.280
is what I was raised with
as above all things.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.480
And that you can fix it
with that.

